 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to exempting certain\nmembers of the armed forces and dependents to operate a motor vehicle or\nmotorcycle on the public highways of this state without being licensed in New\nYork",
    "basePrintNo": "S6234",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6234-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §251, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6234\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to exempting\ncertain members of the armed forces and dependents to operate a motor\nvehicle or motorcycle on the public highways of this state without being\nlicensed in New York\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation provides an exemption for active duty military\nstationed in NY, their spouses and dependents from having to apply for a\nNYS drivers' license after 60 days if they wish to retain their license\nfrom their home state.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends § 251 of the Vehicle and Traffic Law to exempt active\nduty military stationed in New York, their spouses and dependents from\nhaving to apply for a New York drivers' license after 60 days.  It main-\ntains that persons under 18 years of age are still restricted from oper-\nation by New York's laws regarding class DJ and DM licenses.  The\nexemption does not apply to those who had their driving privileges\nsuspended or revoked in another state.\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMost jurisdictions allow active duty military stationed in their state\nto retain their home state drivers license. New York is one of 10 juris-\ndictions that still does not. Under current law, they must apply for a\nNew York license after 60 days, even if they wish to remain legal resi-\ndents of their home state. Many choose to do this to retain certain\nresidency privileges, such as voter registration and \"in-state\" tuition\nbenefits. The bill will allow active duty members, their spouses and\ndependents to retain their home state licenses, putting NY in-line with\n45 other states and jurisdictions. This legislation was introduced at\nthe request of the US Department of Defense.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
